<h1>Log Frame</h1>
<p>Every run writes a log: the framework prints each configuration, each result table, each checkpoint, and
	everything the test itself logs, in colour, into a file next to the suite. The <strong>Log</strong> tab of the
	right pane shows that file for the run selected in the tree, following it while the run is going and keeping
	it afterwards. It is the first place to look when a status is not the one expected, because it holds the
	reasoning the tree summarizes.</p>
<p>The right pane has two tabs, <strong>Configuration</strong> and <strong>Log</strong>. Selecting a test opens
	<strong>Configuration</strong>, the run panel used throughout the tutorial. Starting a run switches to
	<strong>Log</strong>, and selecting a finished run in the tree opens <strong>Log</strong> on that run's file.
	The tab is available only for rows that have a log, that is for test runs; a suite, group or test row without
	a run keeps the tab disabled.</p>

<h2 id="the-log-during-a-run">The log during a run</h2>
<p>Run <strong>Status: Fail</strong> from the <strong>Statuses and Results</strong> group and look at the right
	pane while it runs:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/log-frame/log-during-run.png", "Controller during the Status: Fail run with the Log tab open, its title reading Test Run Status: Fail Status: Running with a spinner, the frame outlined in purple, the log scrolling through case configurations and results, and Auto-Scroll ticked at the bottom", "large") }}
<p>The frame's title names the run and its current status, <strong>Running</strong> with a spinner, and the frame
	is outlined in the status colour, purple while running. The log grows as cases finish, and with
	<strong>Auto-Scroll</strong> ticked the view follows the end. Untick it to read an earlier part while the run
	continues; the view stays where it is until it is ticked again.</p>
<p>The tree and the <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/execution-status-frame/">Execution Status</a> frame
	below the log move at the same time, so the three views tell one story: the tree counts, the status frame
	shows where in the run the controller is, and the log shows what is being done there.</p>

<h2 id="the-log-of-a-finished-run">The log of a finished run</h2>
<p>When the run ends the title takes the final status and the outline its colour, red for
	<strong>Fail</strong>. Select the run in the tree at any later time to bring its log back:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/log-frame/log-test-selected.png", "Controller with the Status: Fail run selected in the tree and the Log tab showing the end of its log: the case result rows, the test result table with Failed Cases 14, and the Failures table listing each failing case with its failure description", "large") }}
<p>The end of a test's log is where the summary lives: the test's result table with its counts, and after it a
	<strong>Failures</strong> table listing every failing case with its description, and a
	<strong>Warnings</strong> table when there were any. Reading a log from the bottom up is usually the quickest
	route: the summary says which cases to look at, and the case's own section above says why.</p>

<h3 id="searching-the-log">Searching the log</h3>
<p>Type into the search field above the log and press <strong>Next</strong> or <strong>Prev</strong> to jump
	between matches:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/log-frame/log-search.png", "Log frame with Failure Description typed in the search field and the first match highlighted in a case result table, with Prev and Next buttons beside the field", "large") }}
<p>The current match is highlighted and the view scrolls to it. Searching for a status word such as
	<code>FAIL</code>, a checkpoint message, or a value from the case table is the way to find one case among
	dozens. Clearing the field returns to the end of the log.</p>

<h3 id="the-log-file">The log file</h3>
<p>The three buttons at the bottom right lead to the file behind the view:</p>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Open Log Folder</strong> opens the folder that holds the log in the file manager, with the log
		selected. The other logs of the same test are beside it.</li>
	<li><strong>Open Full Log</strong> opens the log in its own viewer window. The pane shows a bounded tail of a
		very long log; the viewer shows all of it.</li>
	<li><strong>Browse Log File</strong> opens any log file on disk in the viewer, for example one copied from
		another machine or one produced by a CI run.</li>
</ul>
<p>Logs are written as <code>.ansi</code> files, plain text with the colour codes the viewer renders, so they can
	also be read in a terminal or any editor. The <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/logging/">framework
	reference</a> describes where they are written and how a test logs its own messages.</p>

<h2 id="folding-the-right-pane">Folding the right pane</h2>
<p>The right pane can be folded away to give the tree the whole window. Click the collapse arrow at the top
	right of the <strong>Config &amp; Status</strong> title:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/log-frame/right-pane-collapsed.png", "Controller with the right pane collapsed to a narrow vertical strip labelled Config & Status and the execution tree spread across the window", "large") }}
<p>The pane shrinks to a strip carrying its name, and the tree takes the width, with all its columns in view.
	Click the strip to bring the pane back. The Execution Status and Git Status frames fold with it, since they
	share the pane.</p>

<p>The next page, <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/execution-status-frame/">Execution Status Frame</a>, looks
	at the frame below the log, which shows where a run stands while it is running.</p>
